      "sample_index": 2,
      "text": "We begin by looking at triangle $PQC$ which is right and, as such is an isosceles triangle. Therefore, we know that $PC=QC$. In triangle $PCQ$ we notice that $PC=PX+XP=10$ and $QC=PY+QY=30$ (Note: We are considering adding not subtracting).\nNext, we know that the centroid happens to be located at half way along the median so we can conclude that $PR$ is half of $AB$ and also half of the median so we get the equation. $PR=\\frac{AB}{2}$.\nNext we use the properties of the altitude and bisectors. Recall that $$ h_a=\\frac{\\sqrt{ab(p-b)(p-c)}}{a}, p=\\frac{a+b+c}{2},$$ Additionally, $$ p_a=p_b=p_c=\\frac{GH}{2}.$$ We can determine what $h_1$ is:\n$$p_a=p_b=p_c=\\frac{GH}{2} \\implies p_a=p_b=p_c=\\frac{h_a}{2}$$ We can consider the median so we conclude that the centroid is located at $$\\frac{AB}{3},\\frac{AB}{3},\\frac{AB}{3}$$So, we obtain $$\\frac{PR}{AB}= \\frac{\\frac{AB}{2}}{AB}= \\frac{1}{2}$$ It will follow that $PC=PY + PR$ which translates to $$PC+\\frac{AB}{2}=10+15=25\\Rightarrow PC=5,PY=15-5=10\\iff PX=10.$$ We also know that $$ \\overline{BG}=2\\cdot\\text{alt.=8}, \\overline{HC}=3\\cdot\\text{alt.=12},\\overline{AX}=6; AP=24,\\overline{YB} =10\\overline{AL}=20\\iff\\text{area}= 300/2=150\\eqalign{PA\\overline{AB}&=PB\\overline{AC}&\\iff 8(AB)\\cdot 150 &= 12(AC)\\cdot 150&\\iff 4\\cdot AB&=&3\\cdot AC&\\Rightarrow AC=4\\cdot .\\left(\\frac{AB}{3}\\right)&\\quantity{AB}{}\\cdot AC=\\quantity{m}{}}",
